Goal
Fix a regression in streamlink’s HLS audio selection where --hls-audio-select no longer matches HLS audio tracks by their NAME attribute. Restore the previous behavior so that items in --hls-audio-select can match:
- Language codes (e.g., en, fr, de, es)
- Recognized language names (localized variants handled by utils.l10n)
- HLS #EXT-X-MEDIA NAME values (case-insensitive exact match, trimmed)
Scope and constraints
- Implement a minimal, targeted fix that restores previously working behavior without broadening semantics beyond NAME matching.
- Add only the tests necessary to reproduce and validate the bug fix as per the steps below.
- Do not modify unrelated tests or broaden the feature set.
- Do not commit your edits. We will do it later.
Background and observed behavior
User reports that after updating to streamlink 7.1.3+28.g14761c91, using --hls-audio-select "TV Ton,Originalton" or "--hls-audio-select 'Deutsch,Original'" no longer selects multiple audio tracks by their NAME labels as it did before. Language code selection still works (e.g., "en,fr,it,es"), but name-based selection (e.g., "English,Français,Deutsch,Español") does not.
Acceptance criteria
- When audio-select includes non-language tokens matching the HLS #EXT-X-MEDIA NAME attribute (case-insensitive, trimmed), those audio tracks must be selected.
- Multiple audio tracks can be selected by NAME and by language (union of matches), maintaining the selection order given by the user tokens.
- Language code selection continues working exactly as before, unchanged.
- Selection is limited to the audio tracks belonging to the selected video variant’s GROUP-ID.
- No duplicate audio tracks are added to the external audio list (deduplicate by URI).
- Tests reproducing the bug should fail before the fix and pass after the fix.
Tasks
1) Understand the requirements
- Review the CLI option docs for --hls-audio-select in the repository (look in docs or CLI help) to confirm intended behavior regarding language codes and names.
- Examine the HLS code paths that parse variant playlists and select external audio tracks:
  - src/streamlink/stream/hls/hls.py (look for parse_variant_playlist or where external audio tracks are selected)
  - Search for references to “audio-select”, “--hls-audio-select”, “external audio”, “EXT-X-MEDIA”, “NAME”, and “LANGUAGE”.
2) Write a reproduction test case
- Create a small synthetic HLS master playlist fixture that includes:
  - One or more VIDEO variants referencing an AUDIO group (GROUP-ID).
  - Several AUDIO media entries (#EXT-X-MEDIA:TYPE=AUDIO) with LANGUAGE codes and different NAME values.
  - Example 1: AUDIO entries with LANGUAGE="deu", NAME="Deutsch"; LANGUAGE="mul", NAME="Original"; LANGUAGE="deu", NAME="Klare Sprache".
  - Example 2: AUDIO entries with LANGUAGE="en", NAME="English"; LANGUAGE="fr", NAME="Français"; LANGUAGE="de", NAME="Deutsch"; LANGUAGE="es", NAME="Español".
- Write tests that:
  - Use --hls-audio-select with NAME tokens only ("Deutsch,Original") and assert that both corresponding audio tracks are selected.
  - Use --hls-audio-select with NAME tokens ("English,Français,Deutsch,Español") and assert that all corresponding tracks are selected.
  - Use --hls-audio-select with language codes ("en,fr,it,es") and assert the previous behavior remains unchanged.
- Keep the test scope minimal and isolated to HLS audio selection behavior. Make sure the tests fail on the current code before you implement the fix.
3) Locate the bug
- In src/streamlink/stream/hls/hls.py, identify where audio selection is computed for HLS external audio:
  - Find the logic that builds the list of external audio tracks from the master playlist’s media entries based on --hls-audio-select.
  - Look for lists of “audio_select_codes”, mappings of user tokens to language codes, and existing conditionals that check “media.language”.
- Inspect recent commits around 7.1.3+27.g3a687edb to 7.1.3+28.g14761c91 to identify a regression that may have removed NAME-based matching.
4) Implement the fix
- Extend the audio selection logic to include a match against the HLS #EXT-X-MEDIA NAME attribute when a user token is not recognized as a language code or mapped language name:
  - Normalize both the user token and the media NAME by stripping and lowering (case-insensitive exact match).
  - Only consider NAME matches for media.type == AUDIO and media.group_id matching the selected variant’s AUDIO group-id.
  - Preserve the existing behavior and precedence for language code/name matches. Do not change how language code/name resolution currently works.
- Ensure:
  - Multiple selections are supported; order of selection should follow the order of user tokens.
  - Deduplicate external audio tracks by URI if multiple tokens match the same media entry.
  - No change in behavior for language-code-driven selection.
5) Run the reproduction tests
- Run only the new HLS audio selection tests to verify that they fail before your fix and pass after the fix.
- Then run the entire test suite to ensure no regressions.
6) Clean up
- Remove any temporary artifacts created during testing.
- Keep changes minimal: only your targeted code fix and the new reproduction tests.
Implementation notes and hints
- The most likely place to modify is where external_audio is constructed based on media entries in the HLS master playlist for the selected video variant. Look for conditions that currently only check media.language or mapped language names.
- Introduce NAME matching in a way that does not interfere with existing language code selection logic.
- Be careful about matching only within the correct AUDIO group-id.
- Consider using casefold or lower() for case-insensitive matching; trimming whitespace is necessary. Unicode normalization is optional; direct matching of “Français” should work without extra normalization.
- Add a simple deduplication step keyed by the media’s URI.
Validation commands
- Run the new test module: pytest -k "hls and audio and select and name"
- Run the full suite: pytest
